.TH V32DEV.H 3v32 "October 15, 2026" "v32dev 20261014-dev" "Vircon32 C Library"
.SH NAME
v32dev.h \- v32dev core: channels of the memory card mailbox
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include \(dqv32dev.h\(dq
.PP
.BI "v32dev* v32dev_init( int " channel ", int " class " );"
.BI "void    v32dev_free( v32dev** " dev " );"
.PP
.BI "bool    v32dev_probe( v32dev** " dev " );"
.BI "bool    v32dev_wait( v32dev** " dev ", int " frames " );"
.BI "int     v32dev_state( v32dev** " dev " );"
.BI "bool    v32dev_ready( v32dev** " dev " );"
.BI "int     v32dev_events( v32dev** " dev " );"
.PP
.BI "int     v32dev_type( v32dev** " dev " );"
.BI "int     v32dev_class( v32dev** " dev " );"
.BI "int     v32dev_kind( v32dev** " dev " );"
.BI "int     v32dev_status( v32dev** " dev " );"
.BI "int     v32dev_desc( v32dev** " dev ", int " index " );"
.BI "void    v32dev_setdesc( v32dev** " dev ", int " index ", int " value " );"
.PP
.BI "int     v32dev_avail( v32dev** " dev " );"
.BI "int     v32dev_space( v32dev** " dev " );"
.BI "int     v32dev_read( v32dev** " dev ", int* " buffer ", int " count " );"
.BI "int     v32dev_write( v32dev** " dev ", int* " buffer ", int " count " );"
.PP
.BI "bool    v32dev_send( v32dev** " dev ", int " opcode ", int* " payload ", int " count " );"
.BI "bool    v32dev_sendv( v32dev** " dev ", int " opcode ", int* " first ", int " firstcount ", int* " second ", int " secondcount " );"
.BI "int     v32dev_next( v32dev** " dev " );"
.BI "int     v32dev_recv( v32dev** " dev ", int* " opcode ", int* " payload ", int " max " );"
.PP
.BI "V32DEV_TYPE( " class ", " kind " )"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The v32dev devices give a program a way to exchange data with the computer
that runs the emulator, through the memory card (see
.BR v32dev (3v32)
for the overview). The core in
.B v32dev.h
does the part that is the same for every device: it finds out whether the card
carries a live mailbox and whether a host is serving a channel, and it moves
words through the channel. It knows nothing of what the words mean. The drivers
.BR storage.h (3v32)
and
.BR serial.h (3v32)
include it, so a program that uses them does not include
.B v32dev.h
itself; a program that needs a new kind of device uses it directly.
.PP
Like all Vircon32 libraries it is a single header with no library to link
against, and the compiler emits only the functions a program calls. It
includes
.BR memcard.h ", " misc.h " and " time.h
from the Vircon32 DevTools.
.SS Channels
The mailbox has
.B V32DEV_CHANNELS
(4) channels. A channel is a pair of rings, one that the host fills and the
program empties and one the other way, and 16 control words on each side that
tell who is there. Each ring has
.B V32DEV_RING_WORDS
(2024) slots, one of which is never used, so at most 2023 words wait in a ring.
Which device is on which channel is decided by whoever formats the card and
starts the host. A program refers to a channel by its number, from 0 to 3.
.SS Device classes
The host announces the device on a channel as a
.IR type :
the class in the top 8 bits and the kind in the rest, made with
.BR V32DEV_TYPE( class ", " kind ).
The class says what the device is like, in the manner of unix devices:
.nf
V32DEV_CLASS_NONE     0    nothing
V32DEV_CLASS_BLOCK    1    fixed size sectors, random access
V32DEV_CLASS_CHAR     2    a stream of words, like a terminal
V32DEV_CLASS_PIPE     3    a one way stream with end of file (reserved)
V32DEV_CLASS_SOCKET   4    connections and datagrams (planned)
V32DEV_CLASS_CUSTOM   128  128 to 255, free for any use
.fi
.PP
A program says which class it expects in
.BR v32dev_init ,
and the channel is reported as
.B V32DEV_WRONG
if the host serves another. The kinds are
.B V32DEV_KIND_ANY
(0, a generic device of the class),
.B V32DEV_KIND_STORAGE
and
.B V32DEV_KIND_SERIAL
and
.B V32DEV_KIND_STREAM
(1, in the block, char and pipe classes), and in the socket class
.B V32DEV_KIND_TCP
(1),
.B V32DEV_KIND_UDP
(2) and
.B V32DEV_KIND_NETPLAY
(3).
.SS Probing
All access goes through the
.IR probe ,
which a program calls
.B once on every frame
for each channel it uses. The probe looks at the state of the channel and tells
what happened since the previous one. Calling it more than once in a frame does
nothing: the host only changes what the program sees between frames. The
drivers have their own probe that calls this one, and the functions that wait
call it themselves.
.SS Streams and messages
A channel carries either
.I words
(a stream, with
.B v32dev_read
and
.BR v32dev_write ),
which may be taken or given in any amounts, or
.I messages
(with
.B v32dev_send
and
.BR v32dev_recv ):
a number of words, an opcode that the class defines, and that many words of
payload, published all at once, so that a receiver that sees any of a message
sees all of it. The class of the device decides which one a channel uses. Do
not mix them in a channel.
.SS Timing
The host sees what the program wrote at the end of a frame, and the program
sees what the host wrote at the start of one. A request and its reply therefore
take at least one frame, if the host answers before the next one begins, and
the throughput is limited by the rings (2023 words per channel and direction
for each frame).
.SH FUNCTIONS
.TP
.BI "v32dev_init( int " channel ", int " class )
Create an instance for a channel (0 to 3) that expects a device of the given
class (one of
.BR V32DEV_CLASS_... ,
or 0 to accept any). Returns
.B NULL
if the channel is not valid or memory runs out. The channel is not usable until
a probe says so (see
.BR v32dev_wait ).
.TP
.BI "v32dev_free( v32dev** " dev )
Close the channel, so that the host can tell the program has left, release the
instance and set the pointer to
.BR NULL .
.TP
.BI "v32dev_probe( v32dev** " dev )
Look at the channel for this frame. Returns true when the state changed or
something happened (see
.BR v32dev_events ).
Does nothing, and returns false, if the frame is the same as the previous
probe's. When the channel becomes ready, whatever an earlier run left in its
rings is discarded.
.TP
.BI "v32dev_wait( v32dev** " dev ", int " frames )
End frames, probing each time, until the channel is ready. Gives up after the
given number of frames and returns false. A mailbox needs about 3 frames to be
recognized, and a host may take longer: 600 frames is 10 seconds.
.TP
.BI "v32dev_state( v32dev** " dev )
The state of the channel in the latest probe: one of the
.B V32DEV_
states below.
.TP
.BI "v32dev_ready( v32dev** " dev )
True if the state is
.BR V32DEV_READY .
.TP
.BI "v32dev_events( v32dev** " dev )
What happened in the latest probe, as the bits
.BR V32DEV_EV_OPEN ,
.B V32DEV_EV_LOST
and
.BR V32DEV_EV_RESET .
.TP
.BI "v32dev_type( v32dev** " dev )
The type that the host announces (class and kind in one value), or 0 if the
channel is not ready.
.TP
.BI "v32dev_class( v32dev** " dev )
The class of the type, or 0.
.TP
.BI "v32dev_kind( v32dev** " dev )
The kind of the type, or 0.
.TP
.BI "v32dev_status( v32dev** " dev )
The status of the device as the host reports it: the bits
.BR V32DEV_ST_READY ,
.B V32DEV_ST_ERROR
and
.BR V32DEV_ST_HUP .
0 if the channel is not ready.
.TP
.BI "v32dev_desc( v32dev** " dev ", int " index )
Word
.I index
(0 to 7) of the descriptor that the host fills in to describe the device (for
example, the size of a disk). Reading it costs nothing. What the words mean
depends on the class. 0 if the channel is not ready or the index is not valid.
.TP
.BI "v32dev_setdesc( v32dev** " dev ", int " index ", int " value )
Set word
.I index
(0 to 7) of the request words, which the program fills in to ask something of
the device. The host reads them. What they mean depends on the class.
.TP
.BI "v32dev_avail( v32dev** " dev )
How many words can be read now. A ring index that is out of range means the
ring is corrupt: it is emptied and counted in the
.I errors
of the instance.
.TP
.BI "v32dev_space( v32dev** " dev )
How many words can be written now.
.TP
.BI "v32dev_read( v32dev** " dev ", int* " buffer ", int " count )
Take up to
.I count
words from the host. May return fewer, or 0. Returns how many were taken.
.TP
.BI "v32dev_write( v32dev** " dev ", int* " buffer ", int " count )
Give up to
.I count
words to the host. May take fewer, or 0, if the host is not keeping up. Returns
how many were taken.
.TP
.BI "v32dev_send( v32dev** " dev ", int " opcode ", int* " payload ", int " count )
Send a message of
.I count
words (at most
.BR V32DEV_MSG_MAX ,
2020). Returns false, sending nothing, if there is no room for all of it.
.TP
.BI "v32dev_sendv( v32dev** " dev ", int " opcode ", int* " first ", int " firstcount ", int* " second ", int " secondcount )
Like
.BR v32dev_send ,
with a payload made of two pieces one after the other (for example a request
header and the data it carries), so the program need not copy them together.
.TP
.BI "v32dev_next( v32dev** " dev )
The payload size of the next message, or \-1 if none has arrived. If the ring
does not hold a well formed message it is emptied, counted in
.IR errors ,
and \-1 is returned.
.TP
.BI "v32dev_recv( v32dev** " dev ", int* " opcode ", int* " payload ", int " max )
Take the next message, if there is one. Stores its opcode in
.I opcode
and its payload in
.IR payload ,
and returns the payload size, or \-1 if there was no message. A message with
more payload than
.I max
is discarded, and \-2 is returned.
.PP
Every function that takes a
.I v32dev**
accepts a pointer to
.B NULL
and then does nothing (returning 0, false, or the
.B V32DEV_NOCARD
state).
.SH VARIABLES
.TP
.B struct v32dev
A channel. A program should not need to touch its fields; they are:
.nf
int   channel;     channel number, 0 to 3
int   expect;      class the program expects (0: any)
int   state;       V32DEV_ state, latest probe
int   events;      V32DEV_EV_ bits, latest probe
int   frame;       frame counter at the latest probe (\-1: none yet)
int   emu_beat;    latest emulator heartbeat seen
int   emu_still;   probes in a row with the same emulator heartbeat
int   host_beat;   latest host heartbeat seen
int   host_still;  probes in a row with the same host heartbeat
int   session;     host session when the channel became ready
int   errors;      ring indices found corrupt (they were emptied)
int*  header;      the mailbox header in card memory
int*  host;        host part of this channel
int*  cart;        program part of this channel
.fi
.SS States
Returned by
.BR v32dev_state ,
and the reason a channel cannot be used yet:
.TP
.B V32DEV_NOCARD
0. No memory card is connected. (Reading card memory without a card is a
hardware error on the console, so nothing is read.)
.TP
.B V32DEV_NOEMU
1. The card has no live mailbox: it was not formatted, the feature is turned off
in the emulator, or the emulator does not have the patch. A header on the card
is not enough to tell: the emulator also has to be seen counting frames in it.
.TP
.B V32DEV_NOHOST
2. The mailbox is live but nobody serves this channel: the host has not
announced itself, or its heartbeat stopped.
.TP
.B V32DEV_WRONG
3. A host serves a device of another class than the one the program expects.
.TP
.B V32DEV_READY
4. The channel can be used.
.SS Events
Returned by
.BR v32dev_events ,
about the latest probe:
.TP
.B V32DEV_EV_OPEN
1. The channel just became ready.
.TP
.B V32DEV_EV_LOST
2. The channel stopped being ready.
.TP
.B V32DEV_EV_RESET
4. The host restarted while the channel was ready, so the rings were emptied.
Whatever the program was waiting for is lost and has to be asked again.
.SS Device status
The bits of
.BR v32dev_status :
.TP
.B V32DEV_ST_READY
1. The device works.
.TP
.B V32DEV_ST_ERROR
2. The device has failed.
.TP
.B V32DEV_ST_HUP
4. The other end is gone, and there will be no more data once the ring has been
emptied.
.SS Classes and kinds
.TP
.BR V32DEV_CLASS_NONE ", " V32DEV_CLASS_BLOCK ", " V32DEV_CLASS_CHAR ", " V32DEV_CLASS_PIPE ", " V32DEV_CLASS_SOCKET ", " V32DEV_CLASS_CUSTOM
0, 1, 2, 3, 4 and 128 (to 255): see above.
.TP
.BR V32DEV_KIND_ANY ", " V32DEV_KIND_STORAGE ", " V32DEV_KIND_SERIAL ", " V32DEV_KIND_STREAM ", " V32DEV_KIND_TCP ", " V32DEV_KIND_UDP ", " V32DEV_KIND_NETPLAY
0, 1, 1, 1, 1, 2 and 3: see above.
.TP
.BI V32DEV_TYPE( class ", " kind )
The type value for a class and a kind:
.BI ( class " << 24) | " kind .
.SS Library parameters
.TP
.B V32DEV_EMU_STILL
3. Probes in a row without the emulator's heartbeat changing before the
mailbox is taken as not live.
.TP
.B V32DEV_HOST_STILL
120. Frames without the host's heartbeat changing before the host is taken as
gone (2 seconds).
.TP
.B V32DEV_MSG_MAX
2020. The most payload words in a message.
.TP
.BR V32DEV_CHANNELS ", " V32DEV_RING_WORDS ", " V32DEV_PART_WORDS ", " V32DEV_CONTROL_WORDS
4 channels; 2024 slots in a ring; 2040 words in each part of a channel (the
control words and a ring); 16 control words.
.SS Layout of the mailbox
The addresses and the indices of the words in card memory. A program that uses
the drivers or the functions above does not need them; they are here for those
who write a host or a driver. The complete description is in the protocol
document of the project.
.TP
.BR V32DEV_HEADER ", " V32DEV_HOST_AREA ", " V32DEV_CART_AREA
0x3003C000, 0x3003C040 and 0x3003E020. Where the header, the part written by
the host (4 parts) and the part written by the program (4 parts) start.
.TP
.BR V32DEV_MAGIC ", " V32DEV_VERSION
0x31564544 (the bytes DEV1) and 1. Words 0 and 1 of the header. V32DEV_VERSION
is the version of the mailbox protocol, not of the library.
.TP
.BR V32DEV_HDR_MAGIC ", " V32DEV_HDR_VERSION ", " V32DEV_HDR_CHANNELS ", " V32DEV_HDR_PART_WORDS ", " V32DEV_HDR_EMU_FLAGS ", " V32DEV_HDR_EMU_BEAT
Indices of the header words: 0, 1, 3, 4, 8 and 9. The last two are kept by the
emulator in memory only.
.TP
.BR V32DEV_H_TYPE ", " V32DEV_H_FLAGS ", " V32DEV_H_BEAT ", " V32DEV_H_H2C_HEAD ", " V32DEV_H_C2H_TAIL ", " V32DEV_H_SESSION ", " V32DEV_H_STATUS ", " V32DEV_H_DESC
Control words of a part written by the host (the program only reads them): the
type, the flags (bit 0: a host is attached), the heartbeat, the write index of
the host to program ring, the read index of the program to host ring, the
session (it changes each time the host starts serving), the status, and the 8
descriptor words from index 8.
.TP
.BR V32DEV_C_FLAGS ", " V32DEV_C_C2H_HEAD ", " V32DEV_C_H2C_TAIL ", " V32DEV_C_BEAT ", " V32DEV_C_SESSION ", " V32DEV_C_STATUS ", " V32DEV_C_DESC
Control words of a part written by the program: the flags (bit 0: the program
has opened the channel), the write index of the program to host ring, the read
index of the host to program ring, an optional heartbeat, the session (one more
each time the program opens the channel), the status, and the 8 request words
from index 8.
.SH EXAMPLES
A device of the char class used directly: show on the screen the words the host
sends, as characters, and answer each one with the next character.
.PP
.RS
.nf
#include "video.h"
#include "v32dev.h"

void main()
{
    v32dev* dev = v32dev_init( 1, V32DEV_CLASS_CHAR );
    int [2] text;
    int [1] word;

    text[ 0 ] = 0;
    text[ 1 ] = 0;

    while( true )
    {
        v32dev_probe( &dev );
        clear_screen( color_black );

        if( v32dev_ready( &dev ) )
        {
            while( v32dev_read( &dev, word, 1 ) == 1 )
            {
                text[ 0 ] = word[ 0 ];
                word[ 0 ] = word[ 0 ] + 1;
                v32dev_write( &dev, word, 1 );
            }
            print_at( 10, 10, text );
        }
        else if( v32dev_state( &dev ) == V32DEV_NOEMU )
            print_at( 10, 10, "no mailbox: card or emulator" );
        else
            print_at( 10, 10, "waiting for the host" );

        end_frame();
    }
}
.fi
.RE
.SH NOTES
The probe must run on every frame. The host is declared gone after
.B V32DEV_HOST_STILL
frames without a sign of life, and a program that skips frames can make a slow
host look dead.
.PP
The state of a channel is kept in the card (indices and sessions), not in the
program's memory, so that a program restarted by a console reset, or a host
restarted by its user, finds the channel in a state it can repair: when a
channel becomes ready each side empties the end of the rings that it owns.
.PP
The mailbox occupies the last 64 KiB of the card. A program that keeps its own
data there conflicts with it.
.PP
The library allocates with
.BR malloc ,
so a program that needs it must leave memory for the instances.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR storage.h (3v32),
.BR serial.h (3v32),
.BR v32dev (3v32),
.BR memcard.h (3v32),
.BR vircon32 (3v32)
.SH AUTHOR
Matthew Haas, for the v32io project. The Vircon32 standard library it builds
on was written by Javier Carracedo
.RI ( Carra ).
